# English version of the 10 research questions (same content, natural phrasing)
# Source: Kiyosaki real estate narrative map research

Q1 = "Kiyosaki's $1.2B debt is debt on a portfolio of 1,500 apartment units held jointly with partners; his personal share is only $30-60M, yet he loudly advertises the big number himself on podcasts. What commercial benefit does publicly bragging about high debt give him? Give 1-2 testable hypotheses."

Q2 = "In Shenzhen 700 real-estate agents were arrested for appraisal manipulation (a 5M yuan house appraised at 6.5M to extract loans); in the US homeowners extracted $205B in home equity in 2025 and Fannie Mae flagged LLC-to-cash-out-refinance fraud. The mechanism is structurally similar in both countries, but where do the institutional constraints differ? Which chain likely breaks first?"

Q3 = "New home inventory exceeded 10 months of supply in 2026, weekly price cuts hit 30-40%, and new home sales ran at 51,000/month, yet the official narrative says there is a 'housing shortage'. What single data indicator most cleanly distinguishes a genuine shortage from a buyer shortage? Give the indicator and a threshold."

Q4 = "Sellers who cannot sell are delisting and converting to 'accidental landlords', shifting seller pressure onto the rental market. If many homeowners shift from selling to renting, how will rental market signals change, and who ultimately bears the cost?"

Q5 = "Kiyosaki said 'if it all goes wrong, call my lawyer. Firewall - that's how rich people play the game.' Losses go to the partnership, banks, and buyers. In a scenario of falling home prices plus loan maturity walls, does the LLC firewall really protect him personally? What countermeasures do banks have?"

Q6 = "Kiyosaki is not really selling real estate investing; he is selling the belief that 'using debt is smart'. His education empire is itself the exit channel (sell the belief, believers go buy). If this thesis is true, what signal should be observable in his revenue structure?"

Q7 = "Kiyosaki sold $2.25M of BTC in Nov 2025 (bought at $6,000, sold around $90,000) while repeatedly saying 'the housing market is crashing' and 'the best time to get rich is coming'. Is there a self-interested pattern between his crash calls and his own position changes? How would you verify it using a public timeline?"

Q8 = "Appraisal value is expression, cash closing price is settlement; appraisal manipulation uses the expression layer to extract settlement-layer cash; the $1.2B is an expression-layer number. In this framework, which expression-settlement gap is the ordinary investor most likely to be harvested at? Is this gap currently widening or narrowing?"

Q9 = "Believers in 'good debt' who enter become the liquidity providers; when home prices reverse, the guillotine falls. Current watch is 2/4 signals (home price growth slowing, supply months approaching 2008 levels). What is the most likely transmission sequence from the current signals to a narrative collapse? Where is the first break point?"

Q10 = "The $1.2B story is interest-bearing information - media earns traffic, Kiyosaki earns attention, and latecomers inherit the accumulated 'interest' of believing the good-debt narrative. Why is the media willing to help Kiyosaki spread this framing? How do media incentives and Kiyosaki's incentives align?"
